{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And He gave.</b> The empty-headed marvel what Moses did on the mountain forty days and forty nights, and they do not know whether he remained there with HaShem for that number, and double that doubled twice over, one could not know a thousandth part of the deed of HaShem and His ways and the sod of all the commandments He commanded him. For they think that the deed is the essence, but it is not — only the heart and the deed, and the heart and the tongue are for habituation, as it is written: \"in your mouth and in your heart to do it\" (Deuteronomy 30:14). And our early ones said: HaShem wants the heart. And the root of all the commandments is until one love HaShem with all his soul and cleave to Him, and this will not be complete unless he recognizes the deed of HaShem among the supernal beings and the lower beings and knows His ways. And thus said the prophet: \"But let him who boasts boast in this: understanding and knowing Me\" (Jeremiah 9:23) — then it will be clear to him that HaShem does kindness, justice, and righteousness in the earth (ibid.). And one cannot know HaShem unless he knows his own soul, his spirit, and his body, for whoever does not know the essence of his own soul — what wisdom does he have? Now Moses, who prophesied forty years in the wilderness, and stood in many sodot that HaShem revealed to him on Mount Sinai — he himself said before his death: \"You have begun to show Your servant Your greatness\" (Deuteronomy 3:24). And behold, now He began and showed him the greatness of HaShem, and this is truth, for His greatness has no limit (Psalms 145:3)." "<b>Stone tablets.</b> I have already explained it. And the matter of the scriptural phrase 'the finger of Elohim' is according to human usage, for whatever HaShem desires, by the word of His mouth it comes into being — and 'word of mouth' is explained by way of allegory, like the practice of human kings. And thus: \"For He commanded and they were created\" (Psalms 148:5). And the words of HaShem stand fixed in heaven corresponding to the lower beings, as it is written: \"Forever, YHWH, Your word stands in heaven\" (Psalms 119:89). And the discerning will understand." "<b>Says Abraham the author.</b> I must elaborate on the matter of the calf. Some of the early ones said that Hur was killed, and so Aaron feared. And some say that he feared for Israel — if they killed him, HaShem would slay them all. According to the plain sense, if the deed of the calf was idolatry, why did Aaron fear being killed? For after him died many righteous ones of Israel who did not reach Aaron's level of righteousness by so much as the sole of Aaron's foot in proclaiming the oneness of HaShem. And behold, Daniel's companions were cast into the fiery furnace because they would not bow to the image — so how could Aaron make an idol, when the making is worse than bowing? For Aaron was holy to HaShem and a prophet over Israel, and many commandments were given through him together with Moses his brother. So would Hur be better than he? And if he committed idolatry, Moses was obligated to kill him before the calf-worshippers. Yet Moses prayed on his behalf, and he killed the worshippers. And how could he afterward atone for Israel — he and his seed until the end of all generations? And do not set your mind on the verse \"Take a calf of the herd for a sin-offering\" (Leviticus 9:2), for it is not about the calf — for behold, its like is on the Day of Atonement for every anointed priest: he is commanded to give a bull of the herd for a sin-offering, and we cannot say it is on account of the father's sin, for all the priests were sons of Eleazar and sons of Ithamar, and Phinehas was already born. And furthermore, in that same passage Israel give a goat for a sin-offering, and they made no figure of a goat. Also the red heifer is not to atone for idolatry but only to purify the impure. And all men of investigation agree that HaShem would not choose an emissary whom He knows will in the end commit idolatry, and no prophet is an emissary [if he does so]. And behold, we see that Moses said before his death: \"Your Thummim and Your Urim belong to Your faithful one\" (Deuteronomy 33:8) — and he did not mention the matter of the calf, but nothing was found in him except what was found in Moses, and that is the matter of the waters of Meribah, as I shall explain in its place. And some say that Israel deceived Aaron — that they made a mold of a calf, and Aaron cast the gold into the fire and did not notice. Their proof: \"and I cast it into the fire and this calf came out\" (below, 32:24). And this is not correct, for Aaron — how did HaShem choose him if he was such a fool that simpletons could deceive him? And furthermore, why did he build an altar before it and command that they slaughter sacrifices and burnt offerings? And others said: this is not Aaron the brother of Moses who made the calf. This too is vanity, for the assembly gathered only toward the one who was in place of Moses, and thus it is written: \"Behold Aaron and Hur\" (above, 24:14). And if it were another, why did neither Aaron nor Moses kill him when he came down from the mountain? And he prayed on his behalf — for it is not fitting that a righteous man commit idolatry on account of others so that they not die. Also many say that the meaning of \"a feast to YHWH tomorrow\" is that the calf-worshippers would be killed by the hand of Moses. For the implication of his words is only that after he built the altar before the calf, he commanded to proclaim throughout the camp that they slaughter tomorrow on the altar he built — like \"Bind the festal procession with branches\" (Psalms 118:27). And so they did: \"They rose early the next morning and offered burnt offerings\" (below, 32:6). And thoughts of the heart are of no avail, for one who blasphemes HaShem with his speech is put to death. And thus one who says \"Let us go and worship other gods\" (Deuteronomy 13:7) [is liable]. And one who interprets \"I am Esau your firstborn\" (Genesis 27:19) as: \"I am\" — what I am; but Esau is your firstborn — that is neither peshat nor does it emerge from the wisdom of speech. For if a man asks his fellow in court: \"Are you my fellow to whom I lent such-and-such?\" and he says \"I am,\" he cannot say: \"My thought was: I am his fellow, no more.\" So too Daniel's words to Nebuchadnezzar: \"The dream is for those who hate you\" (Daniel 4:16) — by way of rebuke.\n\nAnd now I shall tell you the opinion of the Gaon [Saadia]. He said that Aaron acted with cunning, as Jehu did; therefore he built an altar and brought hardship upon himself. If so, why was HaShem angry with him? And he answered: because he did not kill them immediately as he did upon his arrival. This too is not correct, for he proclaimed \"a feast to YHWH tomorrow\" — and this name [YHWH] is a proper noun for the glory of HaShem, and no other is mingled with it, unlike the word Elohim which applies to many matters. And furthermore, why did he not mention this in his answer to Moses? For he says: \"and I cast it into the fire\" — and the answer does not match the question.\n\nAnd now I shall begin to explain, briefly, part of the sod. Far be it, far be it that Aaron committed idolatry. Israel too did not seek idolatry, but thought that Moses had died — he who parted the sea of reeds for them, as I explained. For they saw that the manna does not descend on Mount Sinai, and Moses remained there forty days, and no man has the power to live that long without food. For he did not tell them when he would come down, nor did he know, for HaShem said to him: \"Ascend the mountain to Me and remain there until I give you the tablets of the covenant.\" And the word Elohim is an epithet of glory dwelling in the form of a body, and thus they said: \"which shall go before us\" (below, 32:1). And if you set your mind on the first journey, then you will understand this. And it was made for the glory of HaShem; therefore Aaron built an altar before it, and proclaimed that they slaughter tomorrow for the glory of HaShem. And so they did as he commanded. And this is Aaron's answer — that they gathered against him. And as I have informed you, every assembly followed by על is pejorative; and if followed by אל, it is praise — like \"and they gathered to King Solomon\" (1 Kings 8:2). And this is: \"You know the people, that it is in evil\" (below, 32:22) — for I did no evil to them, for they sought only that which would go before them, and for the glory of HaShem I made it. Only because Israel were mingled with the mixed multitude, and this is: \"that it is in evil\" — and he did not say \"that he is evil.\" And a few of Israel thought it was idolatry, and they brought sacrifices and bowed to it and said: \"These are your gods, O Israel.\" And do not marvel because the verse says: \"they have corrupted; they have quickly turned aside\" (below, 32:7–8), for it is not written \"all your people.\" Do you not see that in the matter of the devoted things, none betrayed except Achan alone? And behold it is written: \"Israel has sinned\" and \"also they have transgressed\" and so on, \"also they have stolen, also they have deceived, also they have put it among their possessions\" (Joshua 7:11). And behold, all who worshipped the calf — as idolatry, as they said \"These are your gods, O Israel,\" or such was in their thought — were only three thousand. And they are half a tenth of a tenth of the camp. And the upshot is that it was not done properly according to the path that the simpletons supposed, that it was idolatry. And do not marvel because HaShem was angry with Aaron, for this was because he was the cause, for behold, he was punished together with Moses his brother over the matter of the waters of Meribah, though they did not sin willfully, and it is written of them: \"you did not believe in Me\" (Numbers 20:12), \"you rebelled\" (ibid. 27:14), \"you acted treacherously\" (Deuteronomy 32:51). And the astrologers said that the great conjunction of the two supernal [planets] was in the sign of Taurus, and this is false, for it was only in Aquarius. And according to the way of the science of the stars, the sign of Israel is [thus determined]. And many have fled from this sod, generation after generation; I too have seen likewise, and behold they placed it in the midst of the heavens."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {0 "The phrase 'double that doubled twice over' (וכפל כפלו שנים) is hyper-literal; the idiom may denote an indefinitely multiplied span.", 2 "The concluding phrase 'and behold they placed it in the midst of the heavens' (והנה שמוהו בחצי השמים) is hyper-literal; the reference is obscure. 'חבירי דניאל' resolved as 'Daniel's companions' (Hananiah, Mishael, Azariah). The multiple occurrences of ע\"ג and ע\"ז are resolved as 'idolatry' and 'idolatry/foreign worship' respectively; the abbreviation is ambiguous in some witnesses."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:40:19.064971276Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}